<blockquote>
<p>If a German uses the Auschwitz concentration camp as a background to write an SCP, what do you think? I don't think you will deny that SCP is a horror story based on entertainment.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>First, like ARD said, there would be nothing wrong with it, if the article doesn't see the context of war as &quot;entertainment&quot;. Second, I don't think you get the main idea of the article.</p>
<p>What the author has written here is not &quot;hatred and anger against the Japanese&quot;, but about the symbolism of how the pain of war is still there. Maybe I'm interpreting this wrong, but I see the fact that SCP-4000-4, the one that uses anti-meme to conceal himself, is alive to fight represents the lingering grief. He can't let the memory of war go, which is ironic of his power &#8212; erasing memories. This invisible monster that he is fighting, the one that sunk so many Japanese ships, symbolizes that in war, anyone has to suffer; not just the countries damaged by Japanese troops, but also the troops themselves. Loss is unidentifiable and unforgiving here.</p>
<p>I understand that you might be offended when you see a conflicting representation of history. Same with me too. My country was considered evil just because we were communists, but in reality we are the victims.</p>
<p>Nonetheless, there are things that have been done, things that can't be forgotten.</p>

