			<div class="content" id="post-content-3897450">
									

<p>Seconding Mr Tidal, but with slightly different reasons.</p>
<p>I do want to read some article about Auschwitz (unfortunately I'm not German nor Jewish though), but only high quality ones, which match the depth and complexity of feeling it gives. I'm not a fan of <a href="/scp-1145">SCP-1145</a>, but it's not because I'm descended from those who got indirect atomic exposure. It's rather because it's just a radioactive teddy bear that chases you, lacking any depth beyond that. It's really nothing to do with Nagasaki. Similarly, at this point, Unit 731 in this article looks almost replaceable to me with any group related to Imperial Japan and its allies. Not worth a frown it causes.</p>
<p>Plus, Unit 731 is heavily focused on conventional medicine and BC weapons, and it doesn't feel fitted to go into anomalous enhancement of soldiers nor any esoteric containment effort.</p>
<p>So I'd suggest creating a totally new fictional troop dedicated for anomalous research, or, maybe, shinto-craze religious-sounding body, as an alternative. Human experimentation was not monopolized by Unit 731; while 731 facilities were a hub of experiments, other more obscure groups like <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unit_516" target="_blank">Unit 516</a> existed, and in 1989, human bones worth of more than hundred people were discovered at the site once the army surgeon school stood in Tokyo. Their limbs were mostly severed into some parts, and some skulls had gunshot wounds and sword cuts.</p>
<p>(And the JP branch has <a href="http://scp-int.wikidot.com/groups-of-interest-jp#toc5" target="_blank">Unit Negative Numbers</a>, which was dedicated for one PoI's ambition for immortality.)</p>

