					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/w-asriel" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3317001); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3317001&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606834481" alt="W Asriel" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3317001)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/w-asriel" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3317001); return false;" >W Asriel</a></span> <span class="odate time_1535040109 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">23 Aug 2018 16:01</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3923280">
									

<p>As another Chinese, I would remind you that there are plenty of fictional stories based on those atrocities in WWII. I don't need to list them here - you must have heard of a lot of those movies, TV series, etc. in China. What I want to show as an example is <a href="http://scp-wiki-cn.wikidot.com/scp-cn-306" target="_blank">SCP-CN-306</a>, which is also based on Unit 731 and written by a Chinese. Dislike it as you may, but this at least proves that not &quot;only Japanese will write such a document&quot;.</p>
<p>Personally I don't see why people cannot write fictions that are based on tragic, historical event. As long as the story reminds people of what truely happened, it is totally okay for me.</p>
<p>I posted the Chinese version translated by ashausesall. When I was typing in Shirō Ishii - yes, just as you, disgust and hatred of this name nearly overwhelmed me. But then I saw the introdution of Unit 731, about what they had done and how evil they were in the real history, I began to appreciate the skip. Well.. I don't think who write this down is malicious towards China as you suggested. On the contrary, I would say the author wants the readers to know and remember this war crime.</p>

							</div>

								
